"Archaeology is the study of the human past through the things people left behind. Much of that evidence is under the ground. Year after year, soil, sand, and trash pile up, one layer on top of another. So in ground that has not been disturbed, a deeper layer is older than the layer above it. This rule is called the law of superposition. It lets an archaeologist put finds in order (this pot is older than that knife) even without knowing how many years old either one is. That is why archaeologists dig slowly and write down exactly where each thing lay.": "3feaba01f6ce",
"Evidence is never complete, and people do not always read it the same way. Two witnesses can remember one day differently. Two institutions can give different dates for one culture. People who stood on different sides of an event often tell it in different words. So every lesson in this series has two special cards. “How Do We Know?” shows the evidence behind the lesson and where it is kept. “Different Views” puts accounts that disagree side by side, with their sources, and does not pick a winner. Your part is to ask: Who made this? When? Why? Does anything else agree with it?": "a4f10e90d357",
"Evidence of the past comes in three main kinds. The first is things: a pot, a stone tool, a coin, a house. An object that people made or changed is called an artifact. The second is records that people wrote or drew: letters, diaries, maps, photographs. The third is words passed down by mouth, such as the stories, songs, and memories that older people give to younger people. When someone records those memories in an interview, we call it oral history. A source made at the time of the events is a primary source. A source made later by someone without firsthand experience, such as a textbook, is a secondary source.": "6148583df14f",
"Historians disagree about this, so we show both views.": "69d52d860f72",
