"To put a number on the age, scientists need other tools. One of the best known is radiocarbon dating. It works on things that were once alive, such as wood, bone, or shell, and it reaches back about 50,000 years. The answer is never one exact year. It comes as a range, and it can change when new samples are tested. At the Baxian Caves in eastern Taiwan, earlier tests placed the site between 5,000 and 15,000 years ago. Tests reported in 2009 gave an age of more than 20,000 years. When the evidence changes, good history changes with it.": "2ca8535dd8f7",
