"What are the three main kinds of evidence about the past? Give one example of each.": "1d2f97ca71f3",
"What did you eat for breakfast last Tuesday? You may not remember. Now try a harder question: what did people in Taiwan eat three thousand years ago? Nobody alive today saw it, and nobody at the time wrote it down. Yet historians and archaeologists can still tell us a good deal about the distant past. They do not guess. They work like detectives. They look for evidence, and they say only what the evidence lets them say. This lesson is about that evidence: where it comes from, how it is read, and why careful people sometimes read it differently.": "3a0b6fd4fe7b",
"What did you eat for breakfast last Tuesday? You may not remember. Now try a harder question: what did people in Taiwan eat three thousand years ago? Nobody alive today saw it, and nobody at the time wrote it down. Yet historians and archaeologists can still tell us a good deal about the distant past. They do not guess. They work like detectives. They look for evidence, and they say only what the evidence lets them say. This lesson is about that evidence: where it comes from, how it is read, and why careful people sometimes read it differently. Evidence of the past comes in three main kinds. The first is things: a pot, a stone tool, a coin, a house. An object that people made or changed is called an artifact. The second is records that people wrote or drew: letters, diaries, maps, photographs. The third is words passed down by mouth, such as the stories, songs, and memories that older people give to younger people. When someone records those memories in an interview, we call it oral history. A source made at the time of the events is a primary source. A source made later by someone without firsthand experience, such as a textbook, is a secondary source. Writing is young compared with people. The National Museum of Prehistory in Taitung points to a text written in 1603 as the earliest record of customs in Taiwan. But people were on the island long before that. The oldest culture found so far, called the Changbin Culture, goes back about 30,000 years. The time before written records is called prehistory, and in Taiwan it covers almost all of the human past. Taiwan’s school curriculum says that the island’s earliest inhabitants left no written materials, and it names the two most important paths for tracing them: archaeological finds and oral traditions. Archaeology is the study of the human past through the things people left behind. Much of that evidence is under the ground. Year after year, soil, sand, and trash pile up, one layer on top of another. So in ground that has not been disturbed, a deeper layer is older than the layer above it. This rule is called the law of superposition. It lets an archaeologist put finds in order (this pot is older than that knife) even without knowing how many years old either one is. That is why archaeologists dig slowly and write down exactly where each thing lay. To put a number on the age, scientists need other tools. One of the best known is radiocarbon dating. It works on things that were once alive, such as wood, bone, or shell, and it reaches back about 50,000 years. The answer is never one exact year. It comes as a range, and it can change when new samples are tested. At the Baxian Caves in eastern Taiwan, earlier tests placed the site between 5,000 and 15,000 years ago. Tests reported in 2009 gave an age of more than 20,000 years. When the evidence changes, good history changes with it. Evidence is never complete, and people do not always read it the same way. Two witnesses can remember one day differently. Two institutions can give different dates for one culture. People who stood on different sides of an event often tell it in different words. So every lesson in this series has two special cards. “How Do We Know?” shows the evidence behind the lesson and where it is kept. “Different Views” puts accounts that disagree side by side, with their sources, and does not pick a winner. Your part is to ask: Who made this? When? Why? Does anything else agree with it?": "808271254ed6",
